  </owl:NamedIndividual>
  <owl:Class rdf:about="https://spdx.org/rdf/3.1/terms/Software/Package">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/ns/shacl#NodeShape"/>
    <rdfs:isDefinedBy rdf:resource="https://spdx.org/rdf/3.1/terms/Software"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">Package</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">Refers to any unit of content that can be associated with a distribution of
software.</rdfs:comment>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Refers to any unit of content that can be associated with a distribution of
software.</skos:definition>
    <skos:note xml:lang="en">A package refers to any unit of content that can be associated with a
distribution of software.

Typically, a package is composed of one or more files.

Any of the following non-limiting examples may be (but are not required to be)
represented in SPDX as a package:

- a tarball, zip file or other archive
- a directory or sub-directory
- a separately distributed piece of software which another Package or File uses
  or depends upon (e.g., a Python package, a Go module, ...)
- a container image, and/or each image layer within a container image
- a collection of one or more sub-packages
- a Git repository snapshot from a particular point in time

Note that some of these could be represented in SPDX as a file as well.</skos:note>
    <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://spdx.org/rdf/3.1/terms/Software/SoftwareArtifact"/>
